<main id="main" tabindex="-1"><article aria-labelledby="article-title">
<header class="archive-hero" id="top"><div class="hero-meta"><span>Personal archive / Kelsee Rusinek</span></div><p class="archive-kicker">An inventory of practice</p><h1 id="article-title">The history<br>I <em>left out.</em></h1><p class="subtitle">On imposter syndrome, practice, and accounting for your own life.</p><div class="hero-index" aria-label="Themes"><span>Music</span><span>Art</span><span>Technology</span><span>Being</span></div><div class="archive-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span>RECORD 001</span></div></header>
<div class="intro reading"><p>Excuse me! I have a few things I need to say.</p><p>Lately I’ve been digging through my own history and finding things I’ve been leaving out for years. Some felt unrelated to my career. Some were so familiar to me that I forgot they needed explaining.</p><p>Words like “homeschooled,” “church,” and “artist” can bring a whole story with them before you’ve told your own. I’ve made assumptions about other people that way, too.</p><p>Here’s some of the context I left out.</p></div><nav class="chapter-nav" aria-label="Article chapters"><a href="#chapter-01">01 / Education</a><a href="#chapter-02">02 / Community</a><a href="#chapter-03">03 / Practice</a><a href="#chapter-04">04 / The invisible work</a><a href="#chapter-05">05 / Making</a><a href="#chapter-06">06 / Experiences &amp; systems</a><a href="#chapter-07">07 / Recognition</a><a href="#chapter-08">08 / Returning</a><a href="#chapter-09">09 / The record, continued</a></nav><section class="chapter chapter-01" id="chapter-01" aria-labelledby="heading-01"><div class="chapter-layout"><div class="chapter-marker"><span>01</span><small>Education</small></div><div class="reading"><h2 id="heading-01">I wasn’t homeschooled for religion.</h2><p>I learned through accelerated programs designed specifically for me, built from extensive research, because school didn’t fit my educational needs.</p><p>I followed the rules I had to, like any kid. That part wasn’t homeschooling. It was how I was raised: to care more about values than about the structures I was supposed to follow, and to be stubborn about it.</p><p>Entrepreneurship runs on both sides of my family. I grew up watching people build things of their own, and absorbing the belief that if you didn’t know how to do something yet, you could learn.</p><p>That attitude shaped how I approached music, art, technology, and eventually my work. Looking back, I can see how early it started.</p></div></div></section><section class="chapter chapter-02" id="chapter-02" aria-labelledby="heading-02"><div class="chapter-layout"><div class="chapter-marker"><span>02</span><small>Community</small></div><div class="reading"><h2 id="heading-02">I didn’t go to church for religion.</h2><p>Religion can be controversial. People bring different experiences, beliefs, and assumptions to it. I went to church. Whatever you attach to that, here’s what it meant to me.</p><p>I went for the other kids, and for the music. It was a community built for young people. I was open to what I might find there, and music became something spiritual for me—a way to feel connected to something beyond myself.</p><p>I’m here. I’m breathing. None of us fully know how any of this happened. I have eyes, a mind, and a body that can make a difference.</p><p>To me, that’s the most important thing I can do with my life: pay attention to the world, understand what I can, and help change it for the better. I can’t think of a purpose more valuable than leaving something better because I was here.</p><p>Music became one of the ways I could do that.</p></div></div></section><section class="chapter chapter-03" id="chapter-03" aria-labelledby="heading-03"><div class="chapter-layout"><div class="chapter-marker"><span>03</span><small>Practice</small></div><div class="reading"><h2 id="heading-03">Music has been part of my life for as long as I can remember.</h2><p>I started playing at four. By middle school, I was playing keyboards in a band at church.</p><p>Each week, I learned songs by ear. I listened for the parts and searched the keyboard for sounds that matched. I practiced at home. We rehearsed once or twice a week and played two Sunday services, then did it again the next week.</p><p>That repetition taught me that liking a song and being ready to play it are different things. You have to show up prepared and listen to the people beside you. You learn where your part belongs, when to step forward, and when to leave space.</p><p>In high school, I also played weekly with older, seasoned musicians. We would practice, play a set, and stay afterward to write songs. I sang, and I helped put together layered recordings of my vocals. We built backing tracks and used in-ear monitors. The musicians around me taught me, and I had work of my own to contribute.</p><p>I don’t have a timesheet. But when I add up the rehearsals, Sunday sets, practice at home, and songwriting, it’s a lot of hours that never made it onto a résumé. People heard me play. I’m only now learning to account for the work behind it.</p><p>That’s what hours like those build: an ear that hears an arrangement develop, anticipates a change, and knows when something feels right before it can explain why.</p><p>I didn’t realize how far that listening would follow me. Years later, I was doing much the same thing with technology: listening for the missing part, and understanding how individual pieces work together. The instruments were different. The way I listened wasn’t.</p><div class="practice-loop" aria-label="The weekly practice cycle"><span>Listen</span><span>Find the sound</span><span>Practice</span><span>Rehearse</span><span>Play</span><span>Repeat ↺</span></div><aside class="hours-note"><strong>What hours aren’t you counting?</strong><span>Most of us have years of practice that never made it onto a résumé.</span></aside></div></div></section><section class="chapter chapter-04" id="chapter-04" aria-labelledby="heading-04"><div class="chapter-layout"><div class="chapter-marker"><span>04</span><small>The invisible work</small></div><div class="reading"><h2 id="heading-04">Most of the work happens before anyone hears a note.</h2><p>After high school, I played for a few years at a church that met in a temporary space. Every Sunday meant bringing everything in, setting up tables and chairs, setting up the music, rehearsing, playing the service, and packing it all away. I did that every week for at least a couple of years.</p><p>When I remember those mornings, I remember how much of being a musician happened before anyone heard a note. Most of the work was invisible, and the service depended on it.</p><p>Anyone judging the band that morning only judged the part they heard.</p><p>I didn’t know yet how well that would describe my career.</p></div></div></section><section class="chapter chapter-05" id="chapter-05" aria-labelledby="heading-05"><div class="chapter-layout"><div class="chapter-marker"><span>05</span><small>Making</small></div><div class="reading"><h2 id="heading-05">I’m an artist.</h2><p>In college, I studied digital art at Arizona State University, in a program that only existed for a few years. I took charcoal, photography, and art theory. I worked in video, mixed-media sculpture, screen printing, darkroom negatives, and clay, and I studied color theory. I took art history extensively, and not only painting. Architecture too.</p><p>What I learned was how to learn. I wanted to learn art, and I learned it. Try something. Do you like it? Good. Work with what you like, then move on to the next thing. It also taught me diversity. There are many ways to make something.</p><p class="making-line">Working with my hands taught me that I can build. I have a body. I make.</p><p>I wasn’t learning to make one kind of art. I was learning how materials, structure, composition, and experience relate to each other. When I started building digital experiences professionally, I wasn’t leaving art behind. I was working in another medium.</p></div></div></section><section class="chapter chapter-06" id="chapter-06" aria-labelledby="heading-06"><div class="chapter-layout"><div class="chapter-marker"><span>06</span><small>Experiences &amp; systems</small></div><div class="reading"><h2 id="heading-06">I’ve been breaking computers since I was in diapers.</h2><p>I was pressing keys on a computer keyboard at two, around 1988. That’s ordinary for toddlers now. Back then, it wasn’t. I grew up on a computer with the big floppy disks, and around twelve I built a fan site for Weird Al Yankovic.</p><p>In college, a web art class had me building a Lego version of the seven deadly sins in Flash. The focus was never just code. It was building experiences.</p><p>Still, when I entered the job market after college, I realized how little I knew. I laughed about it. Then I did what I had done with music, and learned as fast as I could. Homeschooling had taught me that early: when you don’t know something, you go find out.</p><p>For more than twenty years since, I’ve built digital platforms, including work for the City of Phoenix, Phoenix Sky Harbor International Airport, and the Arizona Lottery. People see the website. They don’t see the structure underneath, the same way nobody at a Sunday service saw the setup.</p><p>Most of my work in code circles the same questions. What does this need? What changes, and what stays fixed? What happens when the thing that changes moves? Two things can look alike, like renting a vehicle and selling one, and still need different models. They share some parameters and not others.</p><p>Then I decide what to share. If something repeats often enough, it belongs in one place, defined once and used everywhere. But sharing has limits. Make too much global, and one change breaks things everywhere. It’s a thin line.</p><p>It’s classification, the same question I ask about historical artifacts: where does this belong? Look for the patterns. Set the structure, and keep what changes moving through it.</p><p>Then I simplify, because I know I’ll be the one fixing it later. Nothing teaches efficiency like being the person who has to maintain it.</p><p>I test my own work too. Whoever builds something should be the first to break it, because they know where it’s weakest. I feel the same way about music.</p><p>I wasn’t changing disciplines. I was adding instruments.</p></div></div></section><section class="chapter chapter-07" id="chapter-07" aria-labelledby="heading-07"><div class="chapter-layout"><div class="chapter-marker"><span>07</span><small>Recognition</small></div><div class="reading"><h2 id="heading-07">I didn’t know how much of this counted.</h2><p>Some of this history I kept quiet because I didn’t want to sound arrogant. Some of it simply didn’t occur to me to mention.</p><p>I remember an early musical practice where someone started playing a song I knew. I didn’t say anything. I just listened. Then he named the artist and asked if I knew it. The song was actually from earlier in that artist’s history, with a band, and I named the band.</p><p>“Oh,” he said. “So you do know.”</p><p>It’s a small moment, but it was typical, and not only in music. In the industry, I often worked with people who had started before me, and they assumed that meant they had more experience. It’s easy to read someone’s age, or their start date, as a measure of what they know. People did that with me. I’ve done it to others, too. Some of them had been in the field longer. But my access to computers—and my curiosity about them—started unusually early for my generation, long before anyone paid me for the work.</p><p>I rarely said so. Experience doesn’t start the day someone hires you, but I let people believe it did.</p><p>Most of us absorb a distinction between what counts in “the real career world” and everything else. I did too. Playing music at church. Writing songs after a set. Learning by ear. Earning Girl Scout badges by figuring out how to do things. Making things because I wanted to understand how.</p><p>I knew those experiences mattered to me. But when it was time to explain what I could do, I didn’t always treat them as relevant. Sometimes I almost treated them as less real.</p><p>Looking back, I can see how much I learned there—and how often I’ve used it since.</p><p>There were people who taught me and people who gave me opportunities. I’m grateful to them. My own effort belongs in that same account.</p></div></div></section><section class="chapter chapter-08" id="chapter-08" aria-labelledby="heading-08"><div class="chapter-layout"><div class="chapter-marker"><span>08</span><small>Returning</small></div><div class="reading"><h2 id="heading-08">I have air to breathe again.</h2><p>There was a long break from playing, roughly eight years.</p><p>A part of my music life ended badly, and I was hurt. Much of what I had believed, I didn’t believe anymore. I stepped away from writing music, and for a while I barely listened to it at all. I listened to people talking on YouTube instead. Then COVID happened, and I was still hurt. People tried to get me back into music, but I wasn’t ready.</p><p>Then motherhood changed my focus. It’s the most important thing I’ve ever taken on, and it will never be finished. I don’t take anything I do lightly, and I take this most seriously of all. I’m the model now, so I have to make sure the model is solid.</p><p>Now I have some air to breathe again, and I choose to make music with it.</p></div></div></section><section class="quiet" aria-label="The love that remained"><div class="reading"><p class="quiet-lead">Music was the love<br>I never left.</p><p>There was a year or two when I couldn’t even listen. It was the quietest that part of me had ever been.</p><p class="still">But it was still there.</p></div></section><section class="chapter chapter-09" id="chapter-09" aria-labelledby="heading-09"><div class="chapter-layout"><div class="chapter-marker"><span>09</span><small>The record, continued</small></div><div class="reading"><h2 id="heading-09">What the dig turned up</h2><p>I started this dig to account for my own work. It reached much further back than my résumé.</p><p>It made me reconsider what I call imposter syndrome. How accurately can any of us judge ourselves if we keep leaving evidence out of our own record?</p><p>That applies when we judge other people too. We meet them somewhere in the middle. There may be years of practice behind something they make look easy.</p><p>When our own relationship with something is complicated, it’s easy to assume we know what it meant to someone else.</p><p>It applies to you, too. What would your record include if you counted everything?</p><p>I’m here, I’m breathing, and I’ve been practicing for most of it. I’m ready to include that part of the story, and to see what I make with it next.</p></div></div></section><div class="endnote reading"><span>End of this record. More to make.</span><a href="/">Explore kelsee.me ↗</a></div></article></main><?php require __DIR__ . '/../../includes/footer.php'; ?>